          "source": {
            "body": "Definitions for a fictional calculation vocabulary; these do not claim any measurement or service was performed.\n\nSPAN is a distance unit, one hundredth of a metre. It is not a duration.\nBEAT is an elapsed duration equal to two minutes; a minute is sixty seconds. It has no origin shift.\nRATE measures speed: one RATE is one metre per second, not seconds per metre.\nHOT denotes an absolute temperature reading. To express a HOT reading as an absolute kelvin reading, add exactly 273.15 to the displayed HOT number. Its increments are kelvin-sized, but HOT itself is a point, not a temperature difference.\nCOLD denotes an absolute kelvin temperature reading with the same numerical value as canonical kelvin. Zero COLD is a temperature origin, not a temperature interval.\nDELTA denotes a temperature difference, one kelvin per displayed unit. No 273.15 offset applies to DELTA. It is not an absolute reading.\n\nAll conversions here are exact. There are no other qualifications or dependencies.\n",
